<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kalmyks</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kalmyk people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kalmyks are a Mongolic people descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a>, chiefly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torghut">Torghuts</a>, who migrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungaria">Dzungaria</a> to the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> in the early 17th century and founded the Kalmyk Khanate under Russian suzerainty. In 1771 a large part of them, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubashi-khan">Ubashi Khan</a>, set out back to Dzungaria, a journey in which many died. Those who remained live mainly in the Republic of Kalmykia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, the only region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> is the traditional religion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalmyks.jpg" alt="Kalmyks" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bata Badmayevich Erdneyev · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kalmykia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Kalmyks are the only Mongolic people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. They are a branch of the Oirat Mongols, whose grazing-lands spanned parts of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> in 1368 the Oirats struggled for 400 years against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha Mongols</a>, the Ming and the Qing for control of Mongolia; the struggle ended in 1757 with the defeat of the Dzungar Khanate by the Qing. The Kalmyks descend chiefly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torghut">Torghut</a>, with smaller groups of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorbet">Dörbets</a>, Khoshuts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a>. The name is of Turkic origin and means remnant or to remain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the early 17th century the First Altan Khan drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a> west to eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torghut">Torghuts</a> were blocked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> from trading with towns on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> and turned to the new Russian outposts. In 1618 the Torghuts and a contingent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorbet">Dörbets</a>, 200,000 to 250,000 people under their taishi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kho-orluk">Kho Orluk</a>, chose to migrate to the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>. They reached the region in 1630 and encamped on land that had belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan-khanate">Astrakhan Khanate</a> and was claimed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. The Kalmyks became Russian allies, and a treaty to protect the southern Russian border was signed with the Kalmyk Khanate. The Oirats expelled most of the Nogai Horde and made the remaining nomadic tribes their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>.</p><p>After the exodus of 1771 the Kalmyks who stayed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> continued their nomadic life between the Don and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>. The Soviet government set up the Kalmyk Autonomous Oblast in November 1920, with Elista as its capital, and in October 1935 it became an autonomous republic. On 28 December 1943 the Soviet government accused them of collaborating with the Germans and deported the entire population to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> within 24 hours, at night in winter, in unheated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> cars. In 1957 Khrushchev permitted the Kalmyks to return, and Kalmykia again became an autonomous republic in 1958.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Kalmyks embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> in the early 17th century and belong to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school, the Yellow Hat sect. Their clergy trained either in monasteries on the steppe, many of which were felt tents that moved with the tribes, or in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. In the 17th century the Khoshut monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaya-pandita">Zaya Pandita</a> devised Clear Script, based on the vertical Mongol script, to write <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a>. They live mainly in the Republic of Kalmykia between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and the Don, and small communities live in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kalmyks” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early seventeenth century the Oirats were divided and under pressure from the Khalkha Mongols and the Kazakhs; some groups migrated west to the Volga, where they became the <b>Kalmyks</b>, and others moved to the Kokonor region and Tibet as the Khoshut.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dzungar invasion of 1723 drove the Kazakhs from their eastern pastures and from the Syr Darya towns, and although they recovered some ground, fear of the Dzungars and pressure from the <b>Kalmyks</b>, Bashkirs and Cossacks led them to seek protection.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The darker legacy includes the Red Terror, collectivisation, the Gulag, the Great Terror of 1937–1938 and the deportation of whole peoples such as the Chechens, <b>Kalmyks</b>, Crimean Tatars and Volga Germans during the Second World War, whose consequences still shape relations in the North Caucasus and beyond.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1801 the Russian emperor Paul I allowed the sultan Bukey, a son of Nurali Khan of the Junior Juz, to lead some 5,000 families across the Ural River into the steppe between the Ural and the Volga left empty by the <b>Kalmyk</b> departure of 1771.</blockquote><a href="/state/bukey-horde">Bukey Horde</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Kalmyks</b> were pastoral nomads following Tibetan Buddhism, with monasteries and a code of law based on the Mongol-Oirat Code of 1640.</blockquote><a href="/state/kalmyk-khanate">Kalmyk Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
